 **named sources, URLs, dates, and exact numbers/quotes** where available.\n\n---\n\n## (a) Retention / session-frequency-over-time data for Calm, Headspace & meditation apps\n\n### 1. Calm: longitudinal subscriber engagement and abandonment\n\n**Hard data (Calm-specific):**\n\n- **Using mobile meditation app data to predict future app engagement** (Calm)  \n  *McClintock et al., Journal of the American Medical Informatics Association (JAMIA), 2022*  \n  URL:  \n  - https://academic.oup.com/jamia/article/29/12/2057/6717758  \n\n  Key numbers / quotes (hard data):\n\n  - “After the first 100 days, **35.2% of all users stopped using the app entirely**.” (p. 2061)  \n  - “By day 350 after subscribing, **58.0% of users abandoned their use of the app**.”  \n  - “By day 350 … **only 42.0% of users performed any meditations on that day or on any subsequent day**.”  \n  - “We found that meditation app use **declined quickly after subscribing** to the app…”  \n  - They fit exponential-decay style models and conclude:  \n    > “The exponential growth models revealed that an average of **64 days** of observations after subscribing to Calm are needed to reach an accurate prediction of future app engagement… After **64 days** … it can be determined with high predictive performance… whether someone will remain engaged with the app through the end of the year.”\n\n  **Interpretation:** This is a clear “novelty wears off” curve: steep early drop in usage, then a persistent, smaller subset of “persistent” users. The modeling explicitly treats engagement as a decaying process.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Meditation apps (generic): steep early disengagement and 30‑day decay\n\n**Hard data: real‑world meditation app engagement**\n\n- **Real-World Meditation App Engagement: Longitudinal Study**  \n  *2026, JMIR mHealth and uHealth*  \n  URLs:  \n  - Open-access version: https://mhealth.jmir.org/2026/1/e79366  \n  - PubMed Central: https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13245846/\n\n  Study of 655 users of a commercial meditation app (not named, but representative of Calm/Headspace‑type products).\n\n  Key numbers / quotes:\n\n  - “App use was relatively low, with **50% (328/655) of users engaging for a total of 16 minutes or less in the first month** after download (median 16.11, IQR 0–74.51 min).”\n  - “**Fewer than 20% (124/655, 18.86%) of users continued using the app after 14 days.**”\n  - “This study reveals that **half of new meditation app users disengage within the first 2 weeks of use, and the majority disengage by the end of 30 days.**”\n\n  **Interpretation:** This is very concrete evidence that usage decays rapidly in the first 2–4 weeks—exactly a novelty curve in practice.\n\n---\n\n**Hard data: survey synthesis of disengagement rates**\n\n- **Engagement With Meditation Apps: Cross-Sectional Survey of Established Users**  \n  *2026, JMIR mHealth and uHealth*  \n  URL:  \n  - https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12910276/\n\n  This study compiles and comments on real-world disengagement estimates:\n\n  - “Real-world estimates for meditation apps indicate **disengagement rates as high as 94% within the first 2 weeks** [4].”\n\n  **Interpretation:** This is not a single cohort curve, but it cites very high 2‑week churn—again, extreme novelty drop-off.\n\n---\n\n**Hard data: mood check-in feature & generic app usage decay**\n\n- **Evaluation of Mood Check-in Feature for Participation in Meditation Mobile App Users: Retrospective Longitudinal Analysis**  \n  *Baumel et al., JMIR mHealth and uHealth, 2021*  \n  URL:  \n  - https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8105761/\n\n  This paper focuses on a mood check-in feature, but includes generic app-retention benchmarks:\n\n  - “In real-world settings (eg, noninterventions), **rates of app usage also decline rapidly; 71% of users stop using commercially available apps within 3 months of starting a subscription** despite having sufficient initial interest and motivation to purchase an app subscription (which range from US $20 to $70).”\n\n  They cite industry reports (Localytics) for this figure. It’s not specific to Calm/Headspace only, but applies to “commercially available apps,” including meditation apps.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Headspace: engagement tapering over a few weeks\n\n**Hard data in a small sample with prompts**\n\n- **Digital Prompts to Increase Engagement With the Headspace Meditation App: Single-Arm Longitudinal Study**  \n  *Torous et al., JMIR Formative Research, 2022*  \n  URL:  \n  - https://formative.jmir.org/2022/3/e30606/\n\n  Eight participants used Headspace for 30 days, with smartphone-based prompts.\n\n  Key usage pattern:\n\n  - “Over 30 days and across the 8 participants, we counted **298 engagements with the Headspace app** from passively collected smartphone data.”\n  - “Across the 8 participants, we observed that **most engagements… occurred during the first week of the study but subsequently tapered down by week 4.**”\n  - “Across all participants, we observed collectively higher engagement… on days when a prompt was sent… However, **engagement with apps decreased over time.**”\n\n  **Interpretation:** This shows a micro-level novelty curve—highest usage in week 1, tapering by week 4—even under active prompting.\n\n---\n\n**Hard data: Headspace usage over months**\n\n- **The Real-World Impact of App-Based Mindfulness on Headspace Users**  \n  *2024, JMIR (Headspace internal + external researchers)*  \n  URL:  \n  - https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10986332/\n\n  This paper characterizes typical engagement, though not as a pure decay curve:\n\n  - “On average, **members engaged with Headspace content for 18.08 (SD 11.42) days** and 547.40 (SD 1004.81) minutes and started **49.73 (SD 48.14) sessions**.”\n  - “On average, members had **2.42 (SD 1.76) active days per week**, 25.89 active minutes per day, and completed 7.11 sessions per week.”\n\n  These statistics describe how many days people are active; combined with general app-retention benchmarks below, they support the pattern that many users are active only a few weeks.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Calm & Headspace day‑1 / day‑7 / day‑30 retention benchmarks (industry / analytic sources)\n\nThese are **industry analytics / commentary** rather than peer-reviewed studies, but they provide concrete numbers.\n\n**Hard data (analytics / derived metrics):**\n\n- **Meditation App Retention Rates: Headspace, Calm, and …**  \n  *Pauso blog, 2026*  \n  URL:  \n  - https://www.pauso.com/blog/meditation-app-retention-rates  \n\n  Reported retention (likely based on analytics platforms; methodology not fully detailed):\n\n  - Table:  \n    - Headspace: **Day 1: 27%**, **Day 7: 11%**, **Day 30: 4.7%**  \n    - Calm: **Day 1: 31%**, **Day 7: 14%**, **Day 30: 5.2%**\n\n  **Interpretation:** These numbers show the classic novelty curve: roughly one-third of installers return the next day, but only about 5% remain by day 30.\n\n- **Time of Day Preferences and Daily Temporal Consistency for Predicting the Sustained Use of a Commercial Meditation App: Longitudinal Observational Study**  \n  *Chapman University, 2025 (preprint / article)*  \n  URL (PDF):  \n  - https://digitalcommons.chapman.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1337&context=psychology_articles  \n\n  The paper examines time-of-day preferences for meditation on a commercial app. Key data:\n\n  - “Large **reductions in the probability of meditation at any time of day were seen over the first 180 days** after creating an account, but this effect was smallest for morning meditation sessions (**63.4% reduction** vs reductions ranging from 67.8% to …).”\n\n  This is essentially a multi‑month decay curve in session probability, with ~60–70% reduction in that 6‑month window.\n\n  The paper also cites market analytics:\n\n  - “Blacker A. Meditation app user sessions fell **48% from their height in Q2 2020, and are continuing to fall**. Apptopia. 2022 Aug 16. URL: https://blog.apptopia.com/calm-and-headspace-losing-app-engagement”\n\n  **Apptopia blog (market analytics):**  \n  - “Calm and Headspace losing app engagement”, Apptopia blog, 16 Aug 2022  \n    URL: https://blog.apptopia.com/calm-and-headspace-losing-app-engagement  \n\n    They report that total user sessions for Calm and Headspace **fell 48% from their pandemic peak in Q2 2020**. This is macro‑level “novelty wears off” after the COVID boom.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Generic mobile & health app retention benchmarks\n\nThese aren’t meditation-specific but contextualize what “normal” looks like.\n\n**Hard data: generic app retention (Localytics)**\n\n- **Efficacy of the Mindfulness Meditation Mobile App “Calm” to Reduce Stress**  \n  *Huberty et al., PLOS ONE, 2019*  \n  URL:  \n  - https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6614998/\n\n  This clinical trial of Calm references broad app retention statistics from Localytics:\n\n  - “Mobile app usage rapidly declines within the first 90 days of downloading the app [88]. According to a **2018 report by Localytics**,  \n    - **21% of users abandon using an app after only one use**,  \n    - **57% of users stop using the app within the first 4 weeks**, and  \n    - average retention by **90 days is only 29% across all industries**…”\n\n  These numbers reflect a general pattern: steep decline in usage over the first month.\n\n**Hard data: cross‑industry retention benchmarks**\n\n- **App Retention Rates (2026)** – Business of Apps  \n  URL:  \n  - https://www.businessofapps.com/data/app-retention-rates/  \n\n  Example (Android):\n\n  - “Average app retention on Android was **21.1% at day one**, dropping to **2.1% by day 30**.”\n\n- **Mobile App Retention Benchmarks by Industry (2026) – UXCam**  \n  URL:  \n  - https://uxcam.com/blog/mobile-app-retention-benchmarks/  \n\n  They summarize benchmarks:\n\n  - “Day‑30 retention above **5%** is healthy for most B2C apps…”  \n  - For one industry breakdown table: “Day‑30 retention **5–8%**” (typical range).\n\n- **App Retention Benchmarks for 2026 – Enable3**  \n  URL:  \n  - https://enable3.io/blog/app-retention-benchmarks-2025  \n\n  They compile multiple benchmark sources:\n\n  - “According to industry benchmarks from Sendbird and UXCam, the average app retention rate is:  \n    - **Day 1: 25–30%**  \n    - **Day 7: 10–15%**  \n    - **Day 30: 5–7%**”\n  - “For most apps, a good app retention rate is **30% for Day 1**, **Day 7 around 15%**, and **Day 30 between 7–10%**.”\n\n- **Key Adoption Metrics for Wellbeing Tools in 2026 – Andy Nadal (blog)**  \n  URL:  \n  - https://andynadal.com/blog/key-adoption-metrics-for-wellbeing-tools-in-2026  \n\n  Reported wellbeing‑app benchmarks:\n\n  - Table: “Day 30 Retention | **3.7–10%** (Average for wellbeing apps) | **8–12%** (Top performers e.g., Calm/Headspace).”\n\nThese are **commentary / analytic estimates**, but they line up with peer-reviewed and Localytics data: 70–90% of users are gone within 30–90 days.\n\n---\n\n## (b) Statements about engagement decay, habituation, novelty in functional/generative music & health apps\n\n### 1. Explicit “novelty fades” commentary in health apps\n\n**Designing for the Human Brain in Health Apps – Anne‑Kathrin Eiselt (behavioral scientist)**  \n*LinkedIn post, 18 Aug 2026*  \nURL:  \n- https://www.linkedin.com/posts/anne-kathrin-eiselt_paying-for-a-health-app-boosts-engagement-activity-7495470258397290496-MeFk  \n\nKey quotes (commentary):\n\n- “I have seen this pattern throughout my career in behavioral science and digital health: **People often engage at first. The experience feels new.** … Then life intervenes. **Novelty fades.** The same feature asks for the same effort while offering **less psychological value.**”\n- “A purchase creates a motivational event. **Novelty creates attention. Neither produces lasting behavior change on its own.**”\n\nThis is explicit conceptual framing of **novelty-driven engagement that decays**.\n\n---\n\n**Gamification 101: Why Your Fitness App Is Losing Users – Mindster blog**  \n*2026*  \nURL:  \n- https://mindster.com/mindster-blogs/fitness-app-user-retention/  \n\nCommentary, not hard data, but directly about novelty curves:\n\n- “When someone installs a new fitness app, there’s a brief window — usually **3 to 7 days — where they open it consistently.** Not because they’ve built a habit, but because **the app is new.**”\n- “Once that **novelty wears off**, most users simply stop. If your app hasn’t given them a concrete reason to return by **day 10**, it won’t get one.”\n- “The first few days are driven by novelty — the app is new and interesting. Once that fades, most apps have nothing pulling users back… **willpower… runs out fast.**”\n\nThis is conceptual, but maps directly onto the day‑1 / day‑7 / day‑30 retention curves above.\n\n---\n\n**You Added Streaks. Retention Is Still Flat. Here’s Why – MarTechVibe article**  \n*2026*  \nURL:  \n- https://martechvibe.com/article/why-health-app-retention-fails-in-week-one/  \n\nThis article focuses on health/meditation app retention, stressing **early drop‑off**:\n\n- Advises teams to “answer first: **where in the first 14 days does behaviour drop off, and why?**”  \n- Provides a day‑range table for measuring early drop-off (no specific percentages, but clearly frames engagement decay in the first weeks as the central problem).\n\n---\n\n### 2. Calm / Headspace research acknowledging engagement decay\n\nWhile I haven’t seen a Calm or Headspace exec explicitly say “novelty wears off” about their music, several research papers funded or co‑authored by them openly discuss engagement decay.\n\n- **Headspace impact study (2024 JMIR)**:  \n  - As above, they show average users active ~18 days and link **higher engagement** to better outcomes. The implication is that many are **low-engagement or early drop-off users**, and that retaining engagement is nontrivial.\n\n- **Calm stress trial (PLOS ONE, 2019)**:  \n  - By quoting Localytics’ rapid decline stats, the authors highlight that app usage usually **“rapidly declines within the first 90 days”**, and this is part of why Calm’s intervention needed evaluation in real-world behavior change.\n\nThese are more **implicit acknowledgments** of novelty/decay rather than explicit statements about habituation.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Functional/generative music: evidence and claims about habituation\n\nDirect statements from companies like Endel or Brain.fm about **novelty curves or habituation** are scarce in peer-reviewed form; most of what exists is marketing claims.\n\nOne notable example:\n\n- **“Brain.fm gives you streaming music to focus, relax, or …” – LifeTips article**  \n  *2026*  \n  URL:  \n  - https://lifetips.alibaba.com/tech-efficiency/brain-fm-gives-you-streaming-music-to-focus-relax-or  \n\n  This article summarizes Brain.fm’s pitch and claims (not peer-reviewed):\n\n  - “Critically, this effect **persisted across device types (iOS, Android, ChromeOS)… and showed **no evidence of habituation over 21 consecutive days of use.**”\n\n  This is a **claim** that Brain.fm’s focus‑music effect doesn’t habituate over 3 weeks; however, the article doesn’t show detailed longitudinal curves or statistics, and I have not seen the underlying study published in an indexed journal. I would treat this as **company/marketing commentary**, not hard evidence.\n\nFor Endel, I haven’t found published retention curves or official statements about novelty decay in generative soundscapes beyond generic product marketing. Academic work on generative music engagement (see below) is more informative.\n\n---\n\n## (c) Academic work on habituation / boredom with background/focus music & meditation apps\n\n### 1. Habituation to repeating / unfamiliar music (novelty management)\n\n**Psychological and Musical Factors Underlying Engagement with Unfamiliar Music**  \n*Margulis et al., 2018, UC eScholarship*  \nURL:  \n- https://escholarship.org/uc/item/4bn224p3  \n\nThis is experimental work on how listeners engage with novel, repeating musical stimuli—very relevant to **functional/generative focus music**.\n\nKey details:\n\n- Participants were played “novel repeating multi‑instrument stimuli” from a library of >450 items, and researchers measured **listening time** and reasons for continuing/stopping.\n- They explicitly tried to influence “**habituating effects of repeating musical material**” by manipulating **novelty** along musical dimensions.\n- Key finding:  \n  > “**Novelty introduced by instrument entrances was the strongest determinant of listening times**, though harmonic variability and timbral features were also important.”\n\n**Interpretation:**  \n- There is clear evidence that **repetition leads to habituation** and shorter listening, and that introducing new elements (instrument entrances, harmonic variation) prolongs engagement.  \n- This supports the idea that static background/focus tracks will produce a novelty‑wear‑off curve, whereas generative music that injects controlled novelty may maintain engagement.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Longitudinal habituation to background music in work/flow contexts\n\n**The Impact of Background Music on Flow, Work Engagement, and Cognitive Performance – Longitudinal Study**  \n*(2025, open-access, likely psychology/neuroscience journal)*  \nURL:  \n- https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12024392/\n\nThis paper examines Mozart background music’s impact on flow and work engagement **over 30 days**.\n\nKey findings / quotes:\n\n- “A longitudinal analysis revealed a **53% attenuation in Mozart’s flow-enhancing effect after a 30-day familiarization** (B = 0.150 vs. baseline 0.321), though residual benefits persisted.”\n- They propose a hybrid mechanism:  \n  > “These findings… propose a hybrid model where music initially operates through a **novelty-driven dopamine release** before transitioning to **schema-based cognitive priming.**”\n- And directly reference habituation theories:  \n  > “This pattern **partially supports habituation theories** (Mackintosh, 1987; McSweeney & Swindell, 1999; Watts, 1979), suggesting that even limited familiarization (**20 min/day for 30 days**) reduces music’s **novelty-driven dopaminergic responses.**”\n\n**Interpretation:**  \n- This is strong evidence of a **novelty wears off curve in music’s cognitive/flow benefits**.  \n- The effect size drops about half in 30 days, but doesn’t go to zero—so habituation attenuates benefits rather than eliminating them.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Background music and sustained attention / mind wandering over time-on-task\n\nThese are shorter-time-scale (minutes, not weeks) but show how music affects **engagement decay** during tasks.\n\n**The effects of self-selected background music and task difficulty on mind wandering and task focus**  \n*2023*  \nURL:  \n- https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37243700/  \n\nKey points:\n\n- “Background music enhanced **task focus and decreased mind wandering**, compared to silence.”\n- “When examined **over time-on-task**, the presence of music led to **smaller task focus declines and mind wandering increases**, compared to silence.”\n- They conclude that “self-selected music appears to confer a **protective effect on task engagement, especially over time-on-task**.”\n\n**Interpretation:**  \n- This addresses short-term engagement decay (within a single task session).  \n- The key insight is that music reduces the natural decline in focus—but doesn’t eliminate it; there is still **decay over time**, just less steep.\n\n**The impact of background music on sustained attention**  \n*2025*  \nURL:  \n- https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/40991821/  \n\nThis paper runs two experiments (2023 & 2024) on vigilance with liked/disliked music vs silence. Summary:\n\n- They systematically measure performance during a visually taxing vigilance task.  \n- While the abstract doesn’t explicitly mention habituation, these kinds of vigilance tasks typically examine **performance decay over time** and how stimuli modulate it.\n\nThese two studies show how **music interacts with attention decay**; they do not show multiweek habituation curves, but illustrate that **engagement tends to decline even with music**, albeit more slowly.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Meditation apps: longitudinal engagement decay (behavioral data)\n\nThe meditation-app papers discussed under (a) are also directly relevant here as **behavioral habituation data**.\n\n- **Real-World Meditation App Engagement (JMIR, 2026)**:  \n  - Half of users disengage within 2 weeks; majority by 30 days; median first-month usage only ~16 minutes.\n- **Using mobile meditation app data to predict future app engagement (Calm, JAMIA 2022)**:  \n  - 35.2% stop entirely after 100 days; 58% have abandoned by day 350; exponential decay modeling.\n\nThese are essentially **habituation curves at the behavioral level**—users lose motivation/interest to continue sessions.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Attrition in mindfulness app RCTs\n\n**Rates of attrition and engagement in randomized controlled trials of smartphone-delivered mindfulness interventions**  \n*Systematic review, 2023*  \nURL:  \n- https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37862854/  \n\nKey statement (qualitative synthesis):\n\n- “Qualitative synthesis highlighted clear problems with **sustained user engagement with mindfulness apps.**”  \n- Engagement data are “inadequately reported,” but across trials, high attrition and declining engagement are common.\n\nThis is more meta‑commentary, but it confirms the general pattern of **engagement decay** with mindfulness apps.\n\n---\n\n## What is missing or speculative relative to your exact request\n\n- **Endel:** I did not find a peer‑reviewed paper or credible analytics report with explicit **Endel-specific retention curves** (day‑30/day‑90) or statements about habituation/novelty. Most public material is marketing about personalized generative sound without longitudinal usage data.\n- **Brain.fm:** The LifeTips article claims “no evidence of habituation over 21 days,” but this is not backed by a published, peer‑reviewed study that I could locate; treat it as **company-linked commentary** rather than hard evidence.\n- **Formal “novelty wears off” curve models for functional/generative music apps:**  \n  - Meditation apps have multiple **clear curves** (Calm, generic apps) showing steep early drop and long-tail persistence.  \n  - For **focus/generative music apps** (Endel, Brain.fm), comparable multiweek usage curves are not yet widely published.\n\nGiven the current evidence:\n\n- For **meditation and health apps**, there is **strong, concrete evidence** of rapid engagement decay and very low day‑30/day‑90 retention—i.e., a “novelty wears off” curve.\n- For **background/focus music**, there is **solid experimental evidence** of **habituation to repeating music over ~30 days** (Mozart study) and the need for **novel musical events to maintain engagement** (Margulis et al.), plus short-term evidence of partial protection against attention decay.\n- For **functional/generative music apps**, direct longitudinal usage curves are mostly **missing or in unindexed/marketing materials**; we can infer likely habituation from general app-retention data and music-habituation studies, but that is **inference, not hard usage data**.\n\nIf you want, I can help translate these numbers into a stylized “novelty curve” (e.g., an approximate exponential decay model for Calm/Headspace) and compare it to generic app benchmarks.","refusal":null,"reasoning":null,"annotations":[{"type":"url_citation","url_citation":{"url":"https://mhealth.jmir.org/2026/1/e79366","title":"Real-World Meditation App Engagement: Longitudinal Study ...","start_index":0,"end_index":0}},{"type":"url_citation","url_citation":{"url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13245846/","title":"Real-World Meditation App Engagement: Longitudinal Study ...","start_index":0,"end_index":0}},{"type":"url_citation","url_citation":{"url":"https://formative.jmir.org/2022/3/e30606/","title":"Digital 